eq i_{e} , which we refer to as inaccurate step identification . Conversely, when the solution is fully correct, the verifier may make an over-critical mistake , incorrectly rejecting a correct step (a false rejection at step i ^ \hat{i} ). The desired counterpart is a true acceptance , where the verifier correctly judges all steps as correct. Under this view, insufficient strictness leads to under-critical mistakes, while excessive strictness leads to over-critical behavior. 4 Dissecting Step-Wise Verification from Latent Perspective In Section 4.1 , we present a preliminary study on the failure patterns of generative verifiers that motivates our hypothesis. In Section 4.2 , we provide empirical evidence that verifier strictness is indeed steerable via hidden-state intervention. 4.1 Dissecting Verification Failures We examine verification failure cases of Qwen3-8B on ProcessBench using the basic prompt ( Appendix A ), with representative examples shown in Table 1 under the column “baseline incorrect verification”. More examples are shown in Appendix C Table 6 . As shown in Table 1 , the baseline verifier tends to produce a superficial CoT that mainly serves to justify a pre-existing verdict: for under-critical cases, the CoT repeats erroneous intermediate steps rather than independently recalculating; for over-critical cases, it elevates superficial stylistic issues into reasoning errors. These patterns suggest that the verifier’s accept-or-reject tendency may be determined before the corresponding verification paragraph is generated, rather than arising from step-by-step reasoning within it. This aligns with prior work showing that CoT in problem-solving can be “decorative”, with the model’s beliefs are already encoded in the hidden states ( Zhao et al., 2025a ; Boppana et al., 2026 ) . Combined with the sequential structure of step-wise verification shown in Figure 1 , where adjacent verification paragraphs are separated by 

