{	ext{lenient}} . Additional examples are provided in Appendix C . 4.2 Verification Strictness is Adjustable by Hidden-State Steering To test the hypothesis that verifier strictness is steerable, we develop a verification-specific hidden-state steering framework with two stages: an offline stage for constructing a steering vector, and an online stage for test-time intervention. The full pipeline is shown in Figure 1 (b) and elaborated below. Note that this uses a standard activation steering method, contrastive activation addition (CAA) ( Rimsky et al., 2024 ; Wu et al., 2025 ) . Our goal here is not a new steering primitive but to test whether verifier strictness is steerable. We use CAA as a baseline and compare it with our proposed method in Section 5.2 . Extraction of Strictness Steering Vector. We first build a validation set from ActPRM ( Duan et al., 2025 ) . To construct the steering vector for increasing verifier strictness, we use 500 randomly sampled samples labeled as erroneous. For each sample, we use the verifier to generate 16 verification traces with temperature 0.7 and top- p p 0.8. Consider an erroneous solution 𝒮 \mathcal{S} for problem p p , whose annotated first incorrect step is s i e s_{i_{e}} . For each sampled verification trace 𝒱 \mathcal{V} , we segment the trace into { v 0 , … , v n − 1 } \{v_{0},\ldots,v_{n-1}\} using the 

 , and extract the verifier’s final prediction i ^ \hat{i} . We retain only those erroneous samples whose 16 rollouts contain at least one trace with i ^ = − 1 \hat{i}=-1 and at least one trace with i ^ = i e \hat{i}=i_{e} . Recall that a verifier output of − 1 -1 indicates that all steps are correct. This ensures that, for the same problem-solution pair, the verifier exhibits both false-acceptance and true-rejection behaviors across different rollouts. We then collect traces with i ^ = − 1 \hat{i}=-1 . For erroneous solutions, such traces correspond to under-critical failures and therefore imply a false acceptance at the true erroneous step s i e s_{i_{e}} . We then localize the corresponding verification segment v i e v_{i_{e}} using paragraph matching (e.g., “Paragraph i i ”) together with acceptance-oriented keywords such as “correct” or “okay”. We denote the resulting false-acceptance segment by v FA v_{\mathrm{FA}} , and extract the hidden state at the delimiter token 

